line_end only reads; declare it const like its three neighbours.

WHY THE BUILD FAILS:

    ../modules/script.c:268:26: error: initialization discards 'const'
                     qualifier from pointer target type
                     [-Werror=discarded-qualifiers]

`const char *line` at :247, memchr returns `const void *` under glibc's
const-generic macros, and the result is initialised into a `char *`.

memchr, NOT strchr, AND string.h:120 COVERS BOTH:

    # if __GLIBC_USE (ISOC23) && defined __glibc_const_generic && !defined _LIBC
    #  define memchr(S, C, N) __glibc_const_generic (S, const void *, memchr (S, C, N))

So this is the same glibc change as patches/0001 arriving through a different
function. Fixing one file did not fix the package, and would not have been
found by grepping for strchr alone.

const IS THE RIGHT DIRECTION HERE, UNLIKE patches/0001. line_end has exactly
two uses -- an assert that it is non-NULL, and `line_end - line` for a length.
Nothing writes through it. patches/0001 went the other way because tag.c
genuinely mutates its buffer; the test is what the code does with the pointer,
not which function returned it.

THE SAME FILE ALREADY DOES THIS THREE TIMES. script.c:98, :105 and :170 all
assign memchr results to `const char *`. Line 268 is the outlier, so this
matches the file's own convention rather than imposing one.

